## DESCRIPTION
## Chain rule
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Chain rule')

################################################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGchoicemacros.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

################################################################
#
#  Setup

$mc = new_checkbox_multiple_choice();
$mc -> qa(
"Let \( F: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^m \) and \( G: \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}^k \). Fix \(a \in \mathbb{R}^n \). Which of the following are true?",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are differentiable, then \( G \circ F \) is differentiable and \( D(G \circ F)(a) = DG(F(a))DF(a) \).",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are \( C^1 \), then \( G \circ F \) is \( C^1 \) and \( D(G \circ F)(a) = DG(F(a))DF(a) \).",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are \( C^1 \), then \( G \circ F \) is differentiable and \( d(G \circ F)_a = dG_{F(a)} \circ dF_a \)."
);

$mc -> makeLast(
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are differentiable, then \( G \circ F \) is differentiable and \( D(G \circ F)(a) = DG(a)DF(a) \).",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are differentiable, then \( G \circ F \) is differentiable and \( D(G \circ F)(a) = DG(F(a))DF(a) \).",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are \( C^1 \), then \( G \circ F \) is \( C^1 \) and \( D(G \circ F)(a) = DG(F(a))DF(a) \).",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are \( C^1 \), then \( G \circ F \) is \( C^1 \) and \( d(G \circ F)_a = dG_a \circ dF_a \).",
"If \( F \) and \( G \) are \( C^1 \), then \( G \circ F \) is differentiable and \( d(G \circ F)_a = dG_{F(a)} \circ dF_a \)."
);

################################################################
#
#  Text

BEGIN_TEXT
$BR
\{ $mc->print_q() \}
$BR
\{ $mc->print_a() \}
END_TEXT

$showHint = 1;
BEGIN_HINT
Note that in the chain rule we obtain the differential of the composition as a composition of the differentials of the two maps at \( F(a) \in V \) and \( a \in U \) respectively. This immediately rules out two out of five. The remaining three are correct.
END_HINT

################################################################
#
#  Answers

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
ANS( checkbox_cmp( $mc->correct_ans() ) );

BEGIN_SOLUTION
A. Incorrect, \( DG(a) \) is evaluated at the wrong point.
$PAR
B. Correct. Recall that the matrix of the composition of two linear maps is nothing but the product of the corresponding matrices.
$PAR
C. Correct, it is the statement of Corollary 4.1.4.
$PAR
D. Incorrect, \( dG_a \) is evaluated at the wrong point.
$PAR
E. Correct, again by Corollary 4.1.4.
END_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();